program            = 010_anonymous_struct_union
area               = 10_declarations_and_types
description        = Anonymous struct and union members and their name lookup, including a nested anonymous struct inside an anonymous union and lookup through a const-qualified pointer
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
NO TYPE PUNNING. Every anonymous-union member is written and read back through the SAME member,
so no value is ever written through one union member and read through another -- which would be
type punning whose result is unspecified or undefined and would make any divergence prove
nothing about either compiler. This is why there are two separate objects rather than one: u has
as_int written to 16 and as_int read back, and lo and hi are never read from u; halves has lo
and hi written to 5 and 6 and lo and hi read back, and as_int is never read from halves. The
two objects exist precisely so that neither has to be re-purposed.

REMAINING UB-FREEDOM ARGUMENT. Every member that is read is written first. u.tag and u.as_int
are both assigned before the direct line prints them. halves.tag, halves.lo and halves.hi are
all assigned before the halves line prints them. All four members of s -- lead, mid_a, mid_b and
trail -- are assigned before either read_mid or the lookup line reads them. The short
assignments use explicit casts of the small values 5 and 6, both far inside the short range, so
the conversions are value preserving. read_mid takes a pointer to a fully initialized automatic
object and reads only two named members through it, dereferencing a valid address. All
arithmetic is on small int values far from the int range edges, so there is no signed overflow:
the largest value computed is 14. No shift and no pointer arithmetic is performed, and no
pointer is formed one past the end. No object is modified twice between sequence points, and no
printf call receives a side-effecting argument -- read_mid is pure and its result is assigned to
runtime on its own statement first, so unspecified evaluation order cannot affect the output.
There is no aliasing violation: every object is accessed only through an lvalue of its own
declared type. No padding byte is read, and in fact neither aggregate contains any padding at
all -- see the implementation-defined notes.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
NO PADDING AND FULL WIDTH NORMALIZATION. Every member of both aggregates is an int or a short,
so neither struct contains any internal or trailing padding on any of the four targets, and no
padding byte can be printed or compared -- padding contents are unspecified by the standard, so
a program that touched them would generate divergences that say nothing about compiler
correctness. Both printed sizes follow directly: struct outer_u is an int tag plus a union whose
largest member is four bytes wide, whether that is the int or the pair of shorts, giving 8; and
struct outer_s is four ints, giving 16. Both values are identical on x86_64, i686, aarch64 and
riscv64, because int is 4 bytes and short is 2 bytes on all four. No sizeof of long, pointer,
double, long long or any aggregate containing them is printed, so the measured i686 difference
(4-byte long and pointer versus 8 bytes elsewhere) cannot surface. No address or pointer value
is printed. Plain char is not used anywhere in the program, so the measured char signedness
difference (signed on x86_64 and i686, unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64) is irrelevant. The only
printf conversion specifier used in the program is %d.

DOCUMENTATION GAP, RECORDED AS A DECISION RATHER THAN AN OVERSIGHT. Anonymous struct and union
members are not named anywhere in this repository's documentation: the technical specification
covers the C11 keyword inventory, the declaration parser and the type system, but never mentions
anonymous aggregate members specifically. The feature is nevertheless written and executed
here, because excluding a language feature for being undocumented or awkward is forbidden.
Crucially, no expected-divergence marker is attached and no target restriction is applied. The
corpus carries exactly two active markers -- XD-GCCEXT-CASE-RANGES-001, scoped to oracle (a) on one
program in area 08, and XD-TYPE-LONGDOUBLE-001, scoped to oracle (b) on one program in area 13 --
and neither covers this area, this program or any oracle it uses. That is the rule rather than an
accident: a marker's basis must be a repository artifact that documents the limitation, and no such
artifact speaks to anonymous aggregate members. Each candidate that was analysed and deliberately
not minted is recorded in tests/conformance/EXPECTED_DIVERGENCES.md section 4, together with the
reason it was rejected. A
divergence in this program is therefore a genuine finding, to be delivered as a verbatim
reproducer with its recorded minimization status, the captured per-compiler and per-backend
outputs and the exact reproduction commands, and reported rather than patched, instead of an
expected divergence to be excused.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
anon_union_direct=1 16
anon_union_halves=2 5 6
anon_struct_lookup=4 5 6 7
runtime_lookup=14
sizes=8 16
END
